From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kiritantakechi <kiritantakechi@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 12:16:28 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] nfqueue: leave token cleanups past the bound to the janitor

The scheduled cleanups are bounded by the queue's length, and reaching
the bound is fatal ("scheduled cleanup set reached NFQUEUE maxlen"),
but a cleanup outlives the packet it came from: the packet is shed at
once, while its token cleanup waits for the drain. When flows are shed
faster than their staging can be retired, the set fills, however fast
the drain, and honk exits. On an emulated NanoPi R4S under host load,
token cleanups retired together, a retirement still took some 4 ms and
the drain retired about 206 a second, while a stream of 200 new UDP
flows a second that wait for honk's decision was shed at about 200 a
second for ninety seconds, the set holding up to 2,865: a runner a few
per cent slower fills it.

A token cleanup only hastens the retirement of staging the kernel
keeps fenced by its token. Without it, the staging expires 120 s after
the flow's first packet, whose time it keeps, and the flow's packets
are queued and dropped until then, as for the staging of a packet the
kernel's queue dropped and userspace never saw; the janitor then
reclaims it. That costs the flows shed past the bound up to two
minutes, where ending the process takes every flow down, and procd's
respawn limit may keep it down. So a token cleanup the full set has no
room for is left to the staging's expiry and counted (cleanupFull among
the API's NFQUEUE metrics), as the correlator counts the packets it has
no room for (correlatorFull), instead of ending the process. A flow
cleanup always has room: it settles one of the correlator's cells, so
flow cleanups never outnumber them.

Tests: a full set leaves one more token cleanup to the janitor,
counted and without a fatal, and still takes a flow cleanup (with the
fatal bound: the set refuses both). The API's metrics test reads the
counter. The design and API references describe it.

Signed-off-by: kiritantakechi <kiritantakechi@gmail.com>
